II. SETTING UP THE 4D-BBS DEMO

First thing you will need, is the proper hardware.  This
includes an Amiga computer system (any model), a modem (up to
9600 baud), a phone line, and at least 1 floppy disk drive (more
floppy drives or a hard drive suggested).

Next you need to create a clean workbench disk which contains
all the standard AmigaDOS commands and handlers.  Delete any
unused workbench programs, leaving all the system directories
and programs intact.  (devs directory, libs directory, etc.)

On this disk, you will need to make a startup-sequece (in the
's' directory).  This startup sequence will need to assign seven
volumes to certain directories in your system.  These volumes
are as follows:

USERS:  - A directory where all the user file
         information will be stored.
BBS:    - This is were the main program resides,
          along with a few system files.
TEXT:   - A directory were all the bulletin files
         will be found, along with the help files.
FILE:   - A directory where all files that are
         uploaded or downloaded will be stored.
MAIL:   - This is where all the private E-Mail for
         4D-BBS will be stored.
MENU:   - A directory where all the menu files are.
BOARDS: - This is where the message's posted on
         4D-BBS will be stored.

Using AmigaDOS, assign all of the above mentioned volume names
to some directory or disk drive.  For the sake of being
organized, assign all of these to different directories, or even
different disk drives if you wish.  For example, you may want to
have a lot of space for files, in this case, assign 'FILE:' to
its own disk drive or hard drive.

When you have decided where the seven volume names will be
assigned and have created the proper directories, modify the
startup sequence to assign these volumes on every bootup.

There are a few AmigaDOS files that you must have in your 'c'
directory on your boot disk.  These include: sort, rename, arc,
and zoo.  You can obtain arc and zoo at any local Amiga BBS 
system.

The next step involves executing a batch file that was included
with 4D-BBS.  Re-Boot the computer to make sure your new
startup-sequence is working properly, and to assign all the
proper volumes.  Type this at the DOS prompt:
execute setup.bat

When the program finishes, you should have all the data files in
their proper directories.  At this point, you can run the
program, type 4D-BBS.

When 4D-BBS start's up, you will see the waiting screen.  4D-
BBS is waiting for a caller.  To log in, press F1.  You will see
the prompt to press return.  Do so and log in with the name
'SYSOP', using the password 'AMIGAISBEST'.

You are now logged onto the demo of 4D-BBS.  Look around, try
all the functions, enter all the different menus, an generally
explore the BBS.  

There are several help files available in the demo.  Read all
the help files, as they should explain everything you.  These
help files should also be included in you final personalized BBS
setup.

III.  PERSONALIZING 4D-BBS

A.  MAKING MENUS

4D-BBS has 44+ different functions which are available
to you.  These functions can be assigned to any keypress that 
you desire.  For example, you may want the letter 'S' to 
represent the function which shows the time.  This can be done 
by entering this in a menu file:
   s:8
'S' is the keypress, ':' is the delimiter, and '8' is the 
function to execute when the user presses 'S'.

Here is a list of all the functions and the letter/numbers that
represent that function:

0   Exit current menu, dropping to previous menu.
1 * Enter a new menu. (suggested for public). (* = Menu name)
2 * Type a text file.  (type TEXT:*)
3   Page the SYSOP for chat.
4 * Execute external AmigaDOS function.  (Execute *)
5   Show the user listing.
6   Show the days log of callers.
7   Change user's information (for users).
8   Show the time.
9   Show the current menu.
A   Toggle expert menu mode.
B   Networking List.
C   Hang up.
D   Add a number to the BBS list.
E   Show the BBS list.
F   Remove a BBS from the BBSlist.
G   Feedback to the SYSOP (user comment).
H   View inside of an ARC file.
I   View inside of a ZOO file.
J   Global quick scan messages.
K   Change current sub-board.
L   List sub-boards.
M   Send private mail to another user.
N   Hunt for keyword in file catalog.
O   Read your private mail.
P   Post a message to current sub-board.
Q   Edit user macro's.
R   Read messages.
S   Scan message headers.
T   XMODEM download a file.
U   XMODEM upload a file.
V   Voting booth's.
W   Sub-Boards Editor.
X   User editor (SYSOP's only).
Y   System editor (SYSOP's only).
Z   Bulletins editor (SYSOP's only).
! * Enter a new menu (higher access).
@ * Enter a new menu.   \
# * Enter a new menu.    - * = New Menu Name.
$ * Enter a new menu.   /
% * Enter a new menu.
> * Net send.                    \
< * Net receive.                  - * = Net Number.
? * Create net information file. /

The functions which have an '*' after the call letter,
require a filename or other text string to follow the
normal definition.  Here is an example menu with comments:
-----
mainmenu.txt    /* text file to send for menu */
MAIN:           /* prompt to show at this menu */

c:3             /* hit 'c' to page sysop */
s:8             /* 's' will show the time */
?:9             /* '?' will show the menu */
x:a             /* 'x' to toggle expert menu's */
g:c             /* 'g' will log the user off */
h:2 helpme.txt  /* 'h' will show a help file */
b:1 bull.menu   /* 'b' will enter a new menu */
v:v             /* 'v' enters the voting booth */

-----

In the above example, the first line contains the name of
the file which will be shown when a user either enters that
menu, or presses the '?'.  The next line contains the
text string which will be shown as a prompt.  The next line
is a delimiter.  After the delimiter, you may enter as many
keypress and function calls as you wish. The very end of the 
menu file should be a carriage return on a line by itself.

The name of these menu files is very important, because
for one menu to call another, you must use this filename.  

You MUST create a file named 'main.menu' because this is
the menu that is called when a user first logs onto the BBS.

It is suggested that you use names like: 'bulletin.menu', and 
'sysop.menu' for clarity.  Each of these files should be 
accompanied with another text file named as follows:
'bulletinmenu.txt', 'sysopmenu.txt'.  These files are the 
actual text files that are displayed when a user enters that
menu.

For more examples, see the actual menu files included with the 
4D-BBS demo.  These files are found in the MENU: volume.

B. SETTING UP THE SUB-BOARDS

The sub-boards system is defined by another text file.
The name of this text file is 'boards.dat' and can be found in
the BOARDS: volume.

Here is an example file with comments:

-----
4             /* number of sub-boards */
General       /* name of the first sub-board */
50            /* max # of messages in this sub */
For Sale      /* name of second sub-board */
20            /* max # of messages in this sub */
Programming   /* name of the third sub-board */
75            /* max # of messages in this sub */
Help          /* name of the last sub-board */
20            /* max # of messages in this sub */

------

The end of this file should have a carriage return on
a blank line.

The number that follows a sub-board's name determines how many
messages will be held at one time for this message base.  When
the number of messages exceeds this number, ther oldest 
message is deleted.

If you are low on disk space, it is wise to keep this
maximum number low (10-25), because each message entered into
the system takes up room, and storing many messages, will take
a lot of disk space.

C. VOTING BOOTH'S

The Voting Booths work through the voting editor. There are three
voting booths, and when the user selects this option, they will
go through each one. Each booth has its own record of who has
voted, so a user may abort, and vote later. A user may only vote
once.

At this point, you have done all the manual file editing that is
necessary (aside from designing your own menu display files).
The only editing that remains is done with the editors
integrated in 4D-BBS.

If 4D-BBS is already running, exit the program with the close
gadget, and run 4D-BBS again.

D. SYSTEM EDITOR

Enter the system editor using the F4 key and change the
settings to your preference.

The first two things you need to edit are the 'new locks' and
'member locks'. 

The 'new locks' refers to the functions that will be locked
to a user who has just joined the BBS.  For example, if you do
not want a new user to enter a message, you would enter the 
letter 'P' (the letter representation of the 'POST' function) 
in this string.  This will cause the BBS to not allow a new
user to use the POST function.

The 'member locks' refers to the functions that a user will
be locked from when they have become a 'member' of the BBS.
Normaly you will always have the sysop functions locked,
as well as any other function which may give a normal user
to much power with your system.

'new limit' and 'member limit' refer to the time limit
that is given to a user when they either join the bbs as a 
new user, or are promoted to a member.

The last three options that you may change are simple toggle 
switches.  

They include 'sysop avail', which determines if the
system boots up with 'sysop not available', or the 'sysop
paged' message when a user requests a chat.

'alias' is an option that allows your logon prompt to
either print 'Enter your Alias: ' or 'Enter your Name: '.
Toggling alias to the ON position will force the BBS to use the
'Enter your Alias: ' prompt.

'expert' is an option which determines if a new user is
logged into the system with the expert menu's on or off
(expert menu's ON means the menu is only shown when
function 9 is executed, otherwise it is shown after each user
action).

E. BULLETINS EDITOR

Enter the bulletins editor using the F5 key.  You will see
10 filenames appear.  These filenames are bulletin files
that you can create and show when a user logs onto the system.

The date of each bulletin file is very important, because
it determines whether a bulletin is to be  shown one time,
every day for a week, or every time a user logs on.  If a date
of 00/00/00 is given, then the file will never be shown.  This
is how you will disable bulletin files.  If a date of 89/09/15 
is given when the current date is 89/09/10, then the bulletin 
will be shown for 5 days and then no longer be shown when that
date is passed.  If you give it a date such as 99/99/99, then 
the bulletin will be shown forever (because it is impossible to 
ever reach this date).

The time part of each bulletin file is only valid on the
actual expiration day of the file.  When the date associated
with this file is reached, a check with the time is made, if the
current time is less than the time indicated on the bulletin
file, then the file is shown.  If the current time has passed the
time on the file, then the file in not shown.

If you wish a bulletin to only be shown one time for each caller,
then you must set its date to the current date, and  its time to
read 99:99.


When you have finished editing all of these data files, you can
begin to run your BBS. Make sure you have everything exactly the
way you want it before you open it to the public.  The public's
first impression of your BBS will heavily effect it's popularity.

IV.  THE SYSOP'S USER EDITOR

The SYSOP's user editor is a powerful editor which allows a
sysop to edit or create users of the BBS.  

When you first execute the user editor, (with the F3 key) you
will be asked to enter a users name, enter the name of the user
you wish to edit or create.  

If the user you wish to edit already exists, his or her complete
user information will be shown on the screen.  You can edit any
one of these elements by pressing the indicated letter.

All of the elements are self explanitory, exept for one which 
will be discussed in detail.  

If the user does not exist, the user data comes up blank with a
few default settings in place.

The one element which needs explaining is the boards access.  It
is shown near the bottom of the screen and has a series of
letters following it.  These letters represent what type of
access a user has to the existing sub-boards (message bases).

The first character in that string represents the first sub-
board, the second the second sub-board, the third... etc.  

The characters used in this string are important.  These letters
consist of  'RWBSN'.  The letter 'R' means the user has only READ
access to that sub-board.  The letter 'W' means they only have
WRITE access.  The letter 'B' means they have both READ and
WRITE access to that sub-board.  The letter 'N' means the user
has no access at all to that sub-board.  The Letter 'S' means the
user has system capabilities for that sub-board and can delete
messages on it, along with reading and writing. The sub-board in
the 'N' case would not even be shown or displayed to that user.

To edit these parameters, simply select the indicated keypress.
You will then see each sub-board name, and the status that user
has for each sub-board. Select the sub-board number you wish to
change, and enter one of the five stats 'RWBSN'.

V. NETWORKING

As of the current version, networking will not be functional. 
Dale is working on the networking rountines, and should have them
done for the next release. Basically they will allow sysop to 
network together and be able to exchange private and public
messages. This is done by a call up routine, and can either be 
done automatically at a set time (if the destination BBS NET is
a one number call), or it can be done manually through the 4D-
BBS terminal (if the sysop wishes to use PCP or StarLink to send
the NET information).

